# Python virtual environment -- recreate with:  python3 setup.py
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Per-user runtime state -- GUI settings and setup.py's remembered checkbox
# selections are specific to your preferences/machine, not something to
# share or overwrite someone else's copy of. Each computer regenerates its
# own (via the app itself for the first, via setup.py for the second).
# (region.json/name_region.json/popup_marker.json used to live here too --
# retired now that every region/marker lives in a profile under profiles/
# instead of a fixed file next to the script; see the profiles/*.json rule
# below for how those are ignored now.)
gui_settings.json
setup_settings.json

# Full game profiles carry YOUR screen region/name_region coordinates and
# other per-machine settings (see game_profile.py's module docstring) --
# not something to hand to someone else or merge from a pull request. The
# part actually worth sharing between people playing the same game is the
# cast (who sounds like what), which the Cast window's Export Cast button
# writes out separately into profiles/casts/*.json -- deliberately NOT
# caught by this pattern, since "profiles/*.json" only matches files
# directly inside profiles/, not its casts/ subfolder. See CAST_EXPORT_DIRNAME
# and Profile.export_cast()/import_cast() in game_profile.py.
#
# NOTE: this only stops NEW profile files from being tracked. Any profile
# already committed before this line was added stays tracked until you
# explicitly run, for each one:  git rm --cached profiles/<name>.json
profiles/*.json

# Downloaded voice models -- these are large (Piper voices are 60-140MB
# each; Kokoro's model + voices file together are ~350MB) and GitHub
# rejects anything over 100MB outright, so none of this belongs in git
# regardless of size. See README.md's "Better voices" section for where to
# download them from. The ** matches any depth, so this covers both
# voices/model.onnx and voices/kokoro/model.onnx-style subfolders alike.
voices/**/*.onnx
voices/**/*.onnx.json
voices/**/*.bin

# PyInstaller build output (setup.py's "Build standalone .exe" button) --
# the whole point of that button is that everyone builds their own .exe
# from source rather than one being committed or shipped in the repo.
# build/ is PyInstaller's scratch work area; dist/ is where the finished
# app actually ends up. The .spec file is auto-generated fresh every run,
# so it doesn't need to be tracked either.
build/
dist/
game-text-speaker.spec
